  THE GATE -- `ctx-oracle` vs `ctx-same`, which is the index's
  INFORMATION with the table's cost held fixed. Both arms carry the same
  machinery; only one of them knows which lesson is being taught.
  retention +5.367 +/- 2.698 (+1.99 SE)   err after +0.0989 +/- 0.0068 (+14.56 SE)
  -> A PERFECT INDEX DOES NOT PROTECT IT. Gating is refused, and with
     stabilisation already a measured null the standard account of
     catastrophic interference is closed on this creature.

  IS THE MEMORY GONE, OR JUST NOT SPOKEN?
  lesson A's stored tilt in slot 0, taught -> after the gap.
  SIGNED, so a negative share means lesson B wrote A's OPPOSITE
  rather than wiping it -- a magnitude could not tell those apart.
    ctx-oracle (gap writes slot 1)  -0.02342 -> -0.02342  = 1.00 +/- 0.06   sign flipped 0/36  unchanged 3/36
    ctx-same   (gap writes slot 0)  -0.02342 -> -0.01513  = 0.65 +/- 0.06   sign flipped 1/36  unchanged 0/36
  isolation   `ctx-oracle` spends 0.2% of the GAP still filed under slot 0,
              so A's slot takes that share of B's writes. Mean |change| in the
              stored tilt: oracle 0.0%, ctx-same 39.6%.
  -> NEITHER LABEL. `ctx-same` keeps 0.65 +/- 0.06, which spans too much
     to call survived, erased or reversed -- and the sign flipped on only
     1 of 36 seeds. Report the ratio; do not move the threshold to reach
     a verdict.

